WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.293
- If you want to understand
the past 70 years

00:00:02.293 --> 00:00:05.088
of often disastrous US foreign policy

00:00:05.088 --> 00:00:06.798
and military intervention,

00:00:06.798 --> 00:00:11.678
look to the 1953 CIA and
MI6-backed coup in Iran

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:14.472
that removed democratically
elected Prime Minister,

00:00:14.472 --> 00:00:18.226
Mohammad Mossadegh and brought
back to power the Shah,

00:00:18.226 --> 00:00:20.061
an authoritarian dictator

00:00:20.061 --> 00:00:22.188
friendly to US and British interests.

00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:24.607
The British and Americans
didn't anticipate

00:00:24.607 --> 00:00:27.694
that their interference would
ultimately set the stage

00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:31.114
for an Islamic revolution led
by the Ayatollah Khomeini,

00:00:31.114 --> 00:00:33.700
which began the repressive theocracy

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.869
that rules Iran to this day.

00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:37.078
I'm standing

00:00:37.746 --> 00:00:39.456
in front of a filing cabinet

00:00:39.789 --> 00:00:41.291
of a drawer full of documents

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:43.376
the essentially changed

00:00:43.376 --> 00:00:44.294
the fate of my country.

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:46.963
In the new documentary, "Coup 53,"

00:00:46.963 --> 00:00:50.508
director Taghi Amirani argues
that this covert operation

00:00:50.508 --> 00:00:53.470
became the template for
American interventionism

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:54.804
all over the world.

00:00:54.804 --> 00:00:57.849
And like so many future
conflicts in the Middle East,

00:00:57.849 --> 00:01:01.019
it began with the desire
among Western powers

00:01:01.019 --> 00:01:03.438
to protect their oil interests.

00:01:03.438 --> 00:01:07.776
Elected in 1951, Mossadegh
had made good on his promises

00:01:07.776 --> 00:01:09.903
to nationalize the industry.

00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:12.238
- And this didn't go down
well with the British

00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:14.616
who were obviously standing to lose

00:01:14.616 --> 00:01:16.201
a very lucrative resource.

00:01:16.201 --> 00:01:20.246
They've joined forces between CIA and MI6

00:01:20.246 --> 00:01:24.167
and staged a military coup
to overthrow Mossadegh

00:01:24.167 --> 00:01:27.378
and put the Shah back in power,
who was much more amenable

00:01:27.378 --> 00:01:30.423
in doing an oil deal
with the Western powers.

00:01:30.423 --> 00:01:32.550
On paper, it was a huge success.

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.803
It was quick, it was cheap.

00:01:34.803 --> 00:01:36.429
No American lives were lost

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:38.139
and it got the results pretty quickly.

00:01:38.139 --> 00:01:40.391
And they thought, well, we no longer need

00:01:40.391 --> 00:01:42.060
to put boots on the ground.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.812
We don't need to get into wars.

00:01:43.812 --> 00:01:46.815
Don't forget at the time
America was fighting

00:01:46.815 --> 00:01:48.691
a hot war in Korea,

00:01:48.691 --> 00:01:50.610
even considering dropping a nuclear bomb.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:54.739
So this was a trouble free,
easy way of changing leaders

00:01:54.739 --> 00:01:55.573
you didn't like.

00:01:55.573 --> 00:01:58.326
- It didn't turn out
to be so trouble-free.

00:01:58.326 --> 00:02:03.123
In 1979, student protestors
stormed the US embassy in Tehran

00:02:03.123 --> 00:02:06.417
and held 52 hostages for a year and a half

00:02:06.417 --> 00:02:09.420
precipitating one of the
tensest diplomatic showdowns

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:10.920
of the past 50 years.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:12.590
Why do Iranians-

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:14.240
- I don't condone it,

00:02:14.259 --> 00:02:16.511
why do Iranians shout, "Death to America"?

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:19.960
"Death to America," didn't
just come out of nowhere.

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:21.760
There's a very good analysis

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:23.420
of why even the hostages were taken.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:25.900
I don't condone taking
hostages, let's be clear,

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:30.360
but the reason those students
raided the American embassy

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:31.280
was because of this.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:33.040
The '53 coup in Iran

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.260
It became the playbook for future coups.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.420
And in fact, the next year in '54,

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.120
they went to Guatemala and
overthrew Jacobo Arbenz,

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:46.140
and of course, Allende in '73 in Chile.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:50.500
In Chile, it was copper,
in Iran it was oil.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.140
- One of the few US leaders

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:55.240
who understood the idea
of blow back was Ron Paul,

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.280
who underscored the longterm
damage of the 1953 coup

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:03.520
in his famous back and forth
with Rudy Giuliani in 2007

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:05.680
during the Republican presidential debate.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:11.000
- When we went into Iran in
1953 and installed the Shah,

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:12.920
yes, there was blow back.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.160
- Another is Senator Bernie Sanders.

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:19.160
- What would Iran look like today

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:23.440
if their democratic government
had not been overthrown.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:25.080
- With less than two months

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:27.040
before the presidential election,

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.240
Donald Trump, who just last year said

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.720
he was not seeking regime change
in Iran is rattling sabers.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:36.320
Citing on specified intelligence reports,

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:39.240
the president has threatened
that any attack by Iran

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.720
in any form against the United States

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:43.640
will be met with an attack on Iran

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.760
that will be a thousand
times greater in magnitude.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:49.640
- American presidents
really are the product

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:51.720
of the military industrial complex

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:53.720
and whoever calls the
shots in those things.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:55.760
The more American foreign policy machine

00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:00.800
ratchets up the rhetoric and
talks war and regime change.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:02.120
And the more they do that,

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:05.120
the more it backfires back in Iran.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:07.920
Extremists love extremists.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.520
In a way they're made for each other.

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:14.240
The more they crank it up, the
other side will crank it up.

